plans.thesis:
uc7's loop is strictly serial. policy.act blocks inside client.request_agent (agent_speedup_v2_user_check_agent.py:258-290, the call is at :263). The simulator steps only after act returns (evaluate_agent_speedup_v2.py:1440, :1483). Every fragment end forces a call because pending segments are never resumed (agent.py:387; fragments.py:160, :335-337). So the arm is frozen for all 14.8 calls × 15.1 s of a mean success.

I measured the structure of the 205 uc7 handoffs:
- pre-contact checkpoints, 45%: they redirect the next target by more than 20 mm in 23 of 91 cases, so they are genuine decision points;
- stalls, 27%: genuine;
- post-gripper checkpoints, 19%: in 23 of 26 the next move is a free-space run-out (median 87 steps);
- envelope vetoes, 3%: pure waste;
- every call also streams about 56% prose ahead of the action, which costs 2.4-3.4 s of the critical path per call.

Three minimal, general changes cut the critical path without geometry, gates, truth triggers or new thresholds:
- S1: run the request in one background thread with a non-blocking view of client state. On its own this changes no behaviour.
- S2: order the output action-first and dispatch the action once its JSON object is complete.
- S3: make observation_after checkpoints non-blocking for model-committed free-space stages, gated at the model's own next contact or gripper stage, and drop the stage-entry envelope veto.

With the already-verified compact I/O plus a recorder thread (P0), arithmetic on the 11 uc7 raw successes gives a 299.6 s mean (median 268.5 s) and 7/11 runs ≤ 300 s at the historical 15.1 s/call. At today's 21.5 s/call it gives 375.4 s and 1/11. Closing that gap needs about 4 fewer blocking calls per run. That must come from lower per-call latency or fewer stalls and corrections, which this plan does not deliver.

